---
<Base title={title} description={description} path="/guides/how-to-count-days-between-two-dates.html">
<aside class="ad-rail" aria-label="Advertisement"><div class="ad-slot ad-slot-rail"></div></aside>
    <div class="wrap article">
      <p class="note"><a href="/guides.html">Guides</a> / Dates</p>
      <h1>How to Count Days Between Two Dates</h1>
      <p class="lead">Counting days sounds simple until you need an exact answer for a contract, a deadline or a visa. A few rules keep the count accurate.</p>

      <h2>Subtract the dates</h2>
      <p>The number of days between two dates is the later date minus the earlier date. Calendars store dates as counts of days, so this subtraction is exact. For example, from 1 January to 1 March is 59 days in a non-leap year: 31 days in January, then 28 days in February.</p>

      <h2>Count inclusively or exclusively</h2>
      <p>Be clear about whether the start date counts. Most date calculators, including ours, give the gap between the dates. If a notice says "within 30 days of 1 May", the count may include 1 May itself, which makes the deadline one day earlier than a simple subtraction suggests. When the stakes are high, check the wording of the rule or ask the organisation which method it uses.</p>

      <div class="ad-slot" aria-label="Advertisement"></div>

      <h2>Leap years</h2>
      <p>February has 29 days in a leap year. A year is a leap year if it is divisible by 4, except century years, which must also be divisible by 400. So 2024 was a leap year, 2100 will not be, and 2000 was. A calculator handles this automatically, which is one reason to use one rather than counting on your fingers.</p>

      <h2>Adding and subtracting days</h2>
      <p>To find a future date, add the number of days to the starting date. To find an earlier one, enter a negative number. Remember that business days, which skip weekends and holidays, are a different calculation and need their own count.</p>

      <h2>Try it yourself</h2>
      <p>The <a href="../tools/date-calculator.html">Date Calculator</a> shows the days between two dates in days and weeks, and finds the date a set number of days from another, including the day of the week.</p>
      <section class="worked">
        <h2>Worked example</h2>
        <p>From 1 January to 1 March in a non-leap year: January has 31 days and February has 28, so the gap is 31 + 28 = 59 days. In a leap year, February has 29 days, so the gap is 60 days.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="faq-list">
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <details><summary>Does the count include the start date?</summary><p>The calculator gives the difference between the dates. If a rule says a period includes both days, add one to the result.</p></details>
        <details><summary>How do leap years change the count?</summary><p>A leap year has 366 days. Any period that includes 29 February in a leap year is one day longer.</p></details>
        <details><summary>How do I count working days only?</summary><p>Business day counts skip weekends and holidays, so they need a separate calculation.</p></details>
      </section>
